This work introduces a binary particle system featuring hard-core exclusion rules and an asexual reproduction mechanism driven by local majority voting. The evolution of spins is governed by a reversible dynamics that ensures thermodynamic equilibrium with respect to a specific non-equilibrating invariant measure. The paper establishes the long-term stability of two distinct stable states characterized by ordered, either all-ones or all-zeros configurations. Under the additional assumption of a small asexual mutation rate, the system undergoes a phase transition into a regime of coexistence, where both initial states persist alongside each other. Furthermore, the study outlines an upper limit on the vanishing probability of a switch from one of these states to the other, demonstrating its exponential decay relative to system size in the process. Finally, the analysis juxtaposes this specific hard-core model against an equivalent agent-based framework employing update methods of the following:
